  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nLarge plume of cirrus clouds associated with the system to our west\nwill continue to overspread the region today. Will likely begin to\nobserve some mid level clouds expanding across the area as well,\nresulting in more filtered sunshine. Regardless, the return flow and\nsubtle expansion of an H5 ridge will usher in seasonably warm\ntemperatures across the Ohio Valley today. Majority of our counties\nare likely to reach the 80 degree mark.\n\nGuidance has continued to keep the ILN fa dry through today and the\nmajority of Thursday. By Thursday night however, a weak trough will\npropagate through the Midwest region and usher in a band of showers.\nScattered showers will continue into Friday until a cold front \npushes through the fa. Instability will be very marginal, limiting \ncoverage in thunderstorms. Depending on how fast the front pushes \nthrough, rain chances may end by Friday evening, particularly for \nlocations north of the Ohio River.\n\nKey Message 2)\nGlobal models have been pushing the cold front that moves through on\nFriday even farther south into the Tennessee Valley for this weekend.\nAs a result, precip chances are trending lower in our CWA. A surface\nhigh pressure now looks to build into the region, offering drier \nconditions and temperatures that will generally trend below seasonal \nnormals this weekend and into early next week.",
